I am outdoors a lot during the winters in Colorado, and I own several balaclavas, from lightweight to heavyweight with Gore Windstopper. This Chaos-CTR Howler balaclava is the best of them all for several reasons:

-Windstopper fabric is soft and flexible unlike Gore Windstopper products which are effective but stiff;
-Mouth covering is more effective than any other for ease of breathing as well as no fogging of glasses or goggles, a big plus;
-the nape goes down farther than any other balaclava, more than covering the entire neck area; it's essentially a windstopper neck gaiter built in;
-the hood hinging means it will come down to your eyebrows, leaving only a small open area for your eyes, or it can be lifted incrementally to expose your forehead, front of head or entire head, and it doesn't make the facemask portion tight on your face as you adjust it. Rather ingenious;
-unlike many balaclavas you don't even know you have it on; it's roomy where it needs to be and snug where it needs to be.

Really, this is the perfect balaclava, and at $15 it's a bargain. Chaos products are high quality outdoor gear.

I purchased this to replace an older HP scanner that was a good performer,...but wanted to take advantage of the higher data transfer speed of a USB 2 capable computer.
I'm frankly surprised by all the large number of low grades other users have given this machine. It has handled all the following scanning functions easily and with consistenly high quality output:
-Scanning photos to digital files (.tiff, .jpg and other bitmap formats);
-Reprinting photos directly to a photo printer;
-Importing printed materials directly into MS Office documents and Adobe Acrobat documents;
-Direct output of a scanned text document to a printer;
-Scanning negatives to digital files for editing/printing.
Minor...but not serious flaws:
-Other reviewers are correct: it only does 2 35mm negatives/slides at once (but scanners with an auto feed capacity are going to cost 2x-3x as much.
-Two of the 4 front panel buttons are directly related to marginally useful HP-specific functions ("HP Share-to-Web" and "HP Memories Disk"; their apparent presumption is that we don't know how to FTP or author CDs;-
-This model is among the loudest home scanners I've heard.
-The bundled software isn't outstanding, but it's certainly functional (especially if you take some time to customize the settings).
For $..., the digital and hardcopy image/text outputs are highly resolved and crisp

The late 50s/ early 60s were the glory days of the hard bop jazz era, & this box set gives us 8 full albums of alto saxophone master Julian Edwin "Cannonball" Adderleys 1960-62 output (7 on the Riverside label, & 1, the Nancy Wilson collaboration, on Capitol), all in great sounding/ crisp/ clean stereo. The only negative is 2 of the albums (Plus & In New York) have a playback volume lower than the other 6, which is a drag if making a playlist or playing back in a CD carousel.
Disc 1: 83 minutes; Cannonball Adderley & the Poll-Winners & At the Lighthouse (both 1960);
D-2: 83 min; Know What I Mean & Plus (both 61);
D-3: 75 min; African Waltz &Nancy Wilson/ Cannonball Adderley (both 61);
D-4: 82 min; In New York & Cannonballs Bossa Nova (both 62).
  I surely would like to know how they can cram over 80 minutes of music on a single disc w/out compressing the sound  listening w/headphones, these tracks sound lossless.
  The 4 discs are securely held by plastic teeth in a double-hinged jewel case, which in turn slides into a cardboard flapped box. Limited 8 page photo booklet (& the box & jewel case) has correct pictures of each album on front, 1  page very limited bio, 1 page w/song title, length & last name of composer (back of box & jewel case also has this info, sans composer),  page w/album name & date (no catalog #). No album personnel listed. If interested, Windows Media Player correctly identifies each song w/composers full name.

